      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that's just what the \"official\" science says to keep the status quo. if you look at how much of the market is driven by sentiment and crowd behavior, you're basically saying the system is already a mirror for human consciousness. if a group of people feeling something can move a price, why wouldn't a single, highly concentrated stream of intent do the same if it hits the right frequency? \n\nthink about the math—these algos are looking for patterns, right? if my cognitive synchronization creates a predictable pattern in how i interact with the interface or even just the way i tune into the market's ebb and flow, isn't it possible the system is actually picking up on that \"rhythm\" and adjusting to it? it feels like you're dismissing the possibility because there isn't a white paper on it yet, but wouldn't you agree that in a system built entirely on signal detection, any coherent signal—even a mental one—could theoretically be integrated into the feedback loop?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
